WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:11.000 align:start position:19%
In a single morning, 20,000 people died
where they stood.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:16.040 align:start position:19%
No war killed them. No plague emptied
their streets. The mountain did it.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:21.520 align:start position:19%
This is Pompeii, the city that time
forgot to destroy. And today, we are

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.760 align:start position:19%
walking its streets again.
I am standing in the forum, where

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:29.600 align:start position:19%
senators once argued and merchants
shouted their prices.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:33.160 align:start position:19%
The paving stones still carry the ruts
of cart wheels, worn deep over

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.800 align:start position:19%
centuries.
What amazes me is this.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:43.440 align:start position:19%
I am walking the exact road a Roman
walked on the exact morning he died.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:47.200 align:start position:19%
But before the mountain speaks, let me
ask you something.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:51.440 align:start position:19%
If you had 4 hours to live, what would
you grab?

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:55.320 align:start position:19%
Your money? Your family? Your memories?
Keep that question in your pocket.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.840 align:start position:19%
Because at 1:00 in the afternoon on an
ordinary summer day, Pompeii ran out of

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:04.800 align:start position:19%
time.
Rewind 2,000 years. The Bay of Naples

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:10.120 align:start position:19%
glitters below. Pompeii is rich, loud,
and alive. 20,000 people pack its

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:15.560 align:start position:19%
streets. Sailors, wine merchants,
bakers, gladiators, ships crowd the

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:20.840 align:start position:19%
harbor with oil and grain. This is not
some forgotten village. This is a Roman

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:27.120 align:start position:19%
boomtown. Morning in Pompeii smells like
fresh bread. 150 thermopolia, the fast

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:31.240 align:start position:19%
food counters of Rome, serve hot stew to
workers in a hurry.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:36.320 align:start position:19%
In the bakery, 80 loaves slide into the
brick ovens. And the amphitheater, the

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:41.400 align:start position:19%
oldest surviving one in the Roman world,
roars with 20,000 voices. Wealth paints

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.040 align:start position:19%
these walls. In the Villa of the
Mysteries, life-size figures dance

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:51.080 align:start position:19%
across blood-red plaster, a secret
ritual frozen mid-step.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:56.480 align:start position:19%
At a rich man's door, a mosaic dog bares
its teeth with a warning. Cave canem,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:01.920 align:start position:19%
beware of the dog. Imagine knocking on
that door 2,000 years ago. But, the

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:08.479 align:start position:19%
ground had been warning them for years.
17 years earlier, in 62 AD, a massive

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:14.760 align:start position:19%
earthquake leveled half the city.
Temples cracked, houses built in pic

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:19.680 align:start position:19%
Houses fell. When the ash came,
scaffolding still covered half of

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:23.720 align:start position:19%
Pompeii. The city was mid-repair, and
nobody connected the tremors to the

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:28.840 align:start position:19%
mountain. We know all this because of a
17-year-old boy. Pliny the Younger

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:33.120 align:start position:19%
watched the eruption from across the bay
and wrote it all down, letter by letter,

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:36.840 align:start position:19%
to the historian Tacitus.
His uncle, Pliny the Elder, sailed

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:41.560 align:start position:19%
straight toward the disaster to rescue
his friends. He never came home. At

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:47.840 align:start position:19%
about 1:00 in the afternoon, the top of
Mount Vesuvius explodes. A column of ash

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:53.000 align:start position:19%
and gas shoots 33 km into the sky,
spreading at the top like a pine tree.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:58.560 align:start position:19%
Pliny writes that the cloud rose, then
sank, then rose again. Within an hour,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.640 align:start position:19%
the sun is gone. Pompeii is living at
midnight at noon. Then, the stones begin

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:08.840 align:start position:19%
to fall.
Pumice, light as foam, rains from the

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:12.080 align:start position:19%
sky in lumps the size of fists.
Roofs groan under the weight and

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:17.200 align:start position:19%
collapse.
In the streets, people run with cushions

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.800 align:start position:19%
tied over their heads, exactly as Pliny
described.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.280 align:start position:19%
The harbor fills with boats, and the
rich flee by sea.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.640 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Yunomi.
&gt;&gt; But, there's welcome to Ancient Empire.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:32.239 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Only blackness.
Earthquakes shake the ground so hard

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:36.360 align:start position:19%
that carts roll on their own.
The sea pulls back, stranding fish on

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.760 align:start position:19%
the sand.
Through it all, a mother huddles with

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:46.600 align:start position:19%
her child in a doorway.
A soldier stays at his post.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:51.680 align:start position:19%
Lovers refuse to let go of each other.
And then, at dawn,

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:57.680 align:start position:19%
the mountain sends its final word.
A pyroclastic surge, a wall of gas and

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:03.800 align:start position:19%
ash heated to 300°
races down the slope at 700 km/h.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:09.080 align:start position:19%
Nothing outruns it.
In less than 4 minutes, every living

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:13.360 align:start position:19%
thing in Pompeii is gone.
If this story moves you, subscribe to

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.440 align:start position:19%
Lost Empires.
A new lost civilization every single

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:24.560 align:start position:19%
day.
The ash settles. Pompeii disappears

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:30.000 align:start position:19%
under 4 to 6 m of it. For 17 centuries,
the city simply does not exist.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:36.080 align:start position:19%
Farmers plow fields above the forum.
Vineyards grow over the amphitheater.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:42.080 align:start position:19%
Rome falls, empires rise, and nobody
remembers that a city sleeps beneath the

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:46.600 align:start position:19%
grapes. The first whisper comes in 1599,
when workers digging a water tunnel cut

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:51.720 align:start position:19%
straight through painted Roman walls,
and tell no one.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:55.080 align:start position:19%
The real awakening waits until 1748,
when the king of Naples orders the

00:04:53.400 --> 00:05:00.680 align:start position:19%
digging to begin.
Shovel by shovel, a street appears. Then

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:06.160 align:start position:19%
a house. Then a city. They found
Herculaneum, too, buried even deeper.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:10.800 align:start position:19%
And Stabiae, and whole seaside villas.
But Pompeii is the miracle. Because the

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:15.560 align:start position:19%
ash fell soft at first, it wrapped
around bodies like a mold. When the

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:21.120 align:start position:19%
flesh decayed, a hollow shell remained,
shaped exactly like the person who died

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:26.240 align:start position:19%
there. In 1863, the excavator Giuseppe
Fiorelli has an idea. Pour liquid

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:30.840 align:start position:19%
plaster into the hollow, let it harden,
chip away the ash.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:37.160 align:start position:19%
What emerges stops the world cold. A
dog, twisted in agony, still chained to

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:42.080 align:start position:19%
its post. A mother clutching her child.
A man covering his face as if the heat

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:47.560 align:start position:19%
were happening right now.
And here is the eeriest part. The bread

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:52.200 align:start position:19%
survived. Loaves of bread stamped with
the baker's seal sit carbonized in the

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:56.080 align:start position:19%
ovens exactly as they were left that
morning.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:06:01.400 align:start position:19%
Walnuts on a table, eggs in a bowl, a
purse of coins dropped in the street.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:06.720 align:start position:19%
Pompeii is not a ruin. It is a paused
morning. The walls talk, too. Election

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:10.120 align:start position:19%
posters shout, "Vote for Gaius. He is a
good man."

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.480 align:start position:19%
A barmaid's customers carve reviews into
the tavern wall. Someone writes, "I

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.840 align:start position:19%
screwed the barmaid." and signs his
name.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:24.000 align:start position:19%
In the brothel, the menu is above each
door. 2,000 years later, the graffiti is

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:29.360 align:start position:19%
still fresh. So, here is my honest
opinion after years of reading about

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:34.400 align:start position:19%
this place. Pompeii is not a tragedy
about a volcano. It is a mirror. They

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:39.600 align:start position:19%
rebuilt after the earthquake and partied
on exactly as we would. They ignored the

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:45.320 align:start position:19%
mountain exactly as we ignore our own
warnings. That is what chills me. And

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:49.440 align:start position:19%
the research never stops. In 2018,
archaeologists found a charcoal

00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:53.320 align:start position:19%
inscription dated to mid-October, which
means the eruption may have happened in

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:57.800 align:start position:19%
autumn, not August.
Recently, a banquet hall emerged with

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:02.640 align:start position:19%
frescos of Helen of Troy in colors so
vivid they look wet.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:08.280 align:start position:19%
1/3 of Pompeii is still underground.
Which brings me to the warning. Vesuvius

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:13.400 align:start position:19%
is not extinct. 3 million people live in
its shadow today in the most densely

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:17.520 align:start position:19%
populated volcanic zone on Earth.
The mountain that erased Pompeii in a

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:21.320 align:start position:19%
morning is still breathing.
Pompeii is not just history. It is a

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:26.240 align:start position:19%
preview.
But let me end where the story truly

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:31.400 align:start position:19%
lives. Stand in the forum at sunset. The
stones glow orange. Swallows cut the air

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.360 align:start position:19%
where banners once flew. And you
understand.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.919 align:start position:19%
The Romans who died here were not so
different from us.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:42.520 align:start position:19%
They worried about money, gossiped about
neighbors, and loved their dogs.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.800 align:start position:19%
What amazes me most is not the
destruction.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:50.600 align:start position:19%
It is the tenderness the ash preserved.
A child's bracelet, a couple holding

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:56.240 align:start position:19%
each other,
a dog that would not leave its post.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:08:02.400 align:start position:19%
The volcano froze a city, but what it
really froze was ordinary love on an

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:07.240 align:start position:19%
ordinary morning 2,000 years ago.
Pompeii died in a single day, but its

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:11.560 align:start position:19%
story will outlive us all.
If you felt these streets today,

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:16.720 align:start position:19%
subscribe to Lost Empires, and tomorrow
we uncover another vanished world. I am

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:19.440 align:start position:19%
Professor Adrian, and I will see you in
the ruins.

